{"op": "append", "content": "[\u26a0\ufe0f] [2026-01-23 05:34:16 UTC] [Agent: ResearchAgent-Database] [Project: scribe_codebase_audit] CONNECTION MANAGEMENT ANALYSIS COMPLETE - CRITICAL FINDING: NO CONNECTION POOLING. Every database operation creates a NEW connection via _connect() (line 1612) and immediately closes it in finally block (lines 1574, 1586, 1598, 1610). Pattern: _execute/_fetchone/_fetchall all call _connect() \u2192 execute query \u2192 conn.close(). This is EXTREMELY inefficient. WAL mode IS enabled (lines 148, 2430) which helps concurrency, but connection overhead is massive. Each MCP tool call triggers multiple queries, each opening/closing connections. Example: append_entry likely does 3-4 queries = 3-4 connection cycles per log entry! | agent=research; confidence=1; file_analyzed=storage/sqlite.py:1565-1623; reasoning={\"how\": \"Traced _execute implementation from usage sites back to _connect() method. Verified connection lifecycle in all 4 query execution methods. Found zero connection pooling or reuse logic.\", \"what\": \"Every _execute/_fetchone/_fetchall creates connection (sqlite3.connect), executes, closes. No connection reuse. Pattern found: 4 methods (_execute_sync, _execute_many_sync, _fetchone_sync, _fetchall_sync) all implement same open\\u2192query\\u2192close pattern. WAL mode enabled but doesn't mitigate connection overhead.\", \"why\": \"Connection pooling is fundamental performance optimization - this is likely causing significant overhead on every MCP operation\"}; severity=critical; stage=research; priority=medium; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/scribe_codebase_audit/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-01-23T05:34:16.361025+00:00_ff7914f9", "timestamp": "2026-01-23T05:34:16.361101+00:00"}
{"op": "commit", "ref_id": "2026-01-23T05:34:16.361025+00:00_ff7914f9", "timestamp": "2026-01-23T05:34:16.372230+00:00"}
{"op": "append", "content": "[\u2705] [2026-01-23 05:34:25 UTC] [Agent: ResearchAgent-Reminders] [Project: scribe_codebase_audit] Database integration mapped. reminder_history table (SQLite) tracks: session_id, reminder_hash, project_root, agent_id, tool_name, reminder_key, shown_at, operation_status (success/failure/neutral), context_metadata. 3 indexes: session_hash (composite), shown_at (temporal), session_tool (composite). Foreign key cascade DELETE on scribe_sessions. Methods: record_reminder_shown(), check_reminder_cooldown(), cleanup_reminder_history(). Dual-backend: DB-first with in-memory fallback for resilience. | confidence=0.95; indexes=3; reasoning={\"how\": \"Read SQLite schema DDL, searched for reminder_history usage patterns, examined storage method signatures\", \"what\": \"Documented reminder_history schema: 10 columns, 3 indexes, cascade delete, operation_status enum. Identified storage methods for recording/checking/cleanup. Verified dual-backend pattern with graceful fallback.\", \"why\": \"Understand cooldown persistence mechanism and storage backend integration\"}; schema_columns=10; storage_methods=[\"record_reminder_shown\", \"check_reminder_cooldown\", \"cleanup_reminder_history\"]; priority=medium; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/scribe_codebase_audit/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-01-23T05:34:25.858493+00:00_fc04805f", "timestamp": "2026-01-23T05:34:25.858528+00:00"}
