{"op": "append", "content": "[\u2705] [2026-01-23 05:35:38 UTC] [Agent: ResearchAgent-Database] [Project: scribe_codebase_audit] MIGRATION SYSTEM ANALYSIS COMPLETE: Clean idempotent design using _ensure_column() and _ensure_index() utilities. (1) _ensure_column_sync: Uses PRAGMA table_info to check existence, only adds if missing (lines 1406-1417), (2) _ensure_index_sync: Executes CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS statement (lines 1557-1563), (3) Migrations called from _initialise() ensuring they run on every startup, (4) Example migration: migrate_add_docs_json_column uses same pattern (lines 1445-1470). DESIGN STRENGTH: Migrations are safe, idempotent, version-control-friendly. WEAKNESS: Every migration opens/closes connection (same pooling issue), but this is startup-only so not critical. | agent=research; confidence=1; file_analyzed=storage/sqlite.py:1403-1563; reasoning={\"how\": \"Traced migration method implementations, analyzed idempotency guarantees, verified integration with _initialise(), assessed connection overhead impact (startup-only = acceptable).\", \"what\": \"Two migration utilities: _ensure_column (PRAGMA table_info check + ALTER TABLE), _ensure_index (CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS). Both idempotent. Pattern: check existence, add if missing, commit. Migration calling convention: from _initialise() on every startup. Connection management: same open/close pattern but acceptable for infrequent startup operations.\", \"why\": \"Migration system quality determines schema evolution safety and deployment reliability\"}; stage=research; priority=medium; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/scribe_codebase_audit/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-01-23T05:35:38.743804+00:00_0ba37c59", "timestamp": "2026-01-23T05:35:38.743856+00:00"}
