{"op": "append", "content": "[\u26a0\ufe0f] [2026-01-23 05:37:08 UTC] [Agent: ResearchAgent-Database] [Project: scribe_codebase_audit] POSTGRES COMPATIBILITY ANALYSIS COMPLETE: MASSIVE DISPARITY. PostgreSQL implementation (260 lines) has CONNECTION POOLING via asyncpg.create_pool (lines 205-210, min=1, max=10, timeout=30s), uses pool.acquire() pattern for all queries (line 220). SQLite implementation (2666 lines, 10x larger!) opens/closes connection per query. FEATURE PARITY: Postgres delegates to scribe_mcp.db.ops module for queries, cleaner architecture. SQLite implements everything inline. CRITICAL GAP: Many SQLite features NOT in Postgres (18 methods vs 73 in SQLite). Postgres is INCOMPLETE - missing: query_entries, dev_plans, phases, milestones, benchmarks, checklists, performance_metrics, document_sections, agent_sessions (full API), reminder_history, bridges. Postgres appears to be EARLY PROTOTYPE or ABANDONED. | agent=research; confidence=1; postgres_lines=260; postgres_methods=18; reasoning={\"how\": \"Compared file sizes (2666 vs 260 lines), counted methods via structure extraction (73 vs 18), identified pooling implementation (_ensure_pool vs _connect), verified missing method implementations via absent imports to scribe_mcp.db.ops.\", \"what\": \"Postgres has connection pooling (asyncpg.Pool, min=1 max=10), delegates to db/ops module, cleaner architecture. SQLite has no pooling, implements inline, 10x more code. Postgres missing 55+ methods that SQLite has. Feature gap includes: query_entries (search), dev_plans tracking, phases/milestones, benchmarks, checklists, document_sections, session management (partial), reminder system, bridge system.\", \"why\": \"Production users expect feature parity between storage backends - incomplete Postgres support blocks adoption\"}; severity=critical; sqlite_lines=2666; sqlite_methods=73; stage=research; priority=medium; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/scribe_codebase_audit/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-01-23T05:37:08.106925+00:00_2e7ad3d5", "timestamp": "2026-01-23T05:37:08.106972+00:00"}
{"op": "commit", "ref_id": "2026-01-23T05:37:08.106925+00:00_2e7ad3d5", "timestamp": "2026-01-23T05:37:08.118504+00:00"}
{"op": "append", "content": "[\u2139\ufe0f] [2026-01-23 05:37:23 UTC] [Agent: ResearchAgent-Database] [Project: scribe_codebase_audit] QUERY PATTERN ANALYSIS: No JOIN queries found in entire codebase - all queries use single-table SELECTs. This is both good (simplicity, no N+1 risk from missing joins) and limiting (cannot efficiently query cross-table analytics). Example: list_projects fetches projects only, no entry counts or metrics in single query. Most queries use project_id filtering with ORDER BY ts_iso DESC pattern. Composite indexes align well with this pattern. EFFICIENCY FINDING: No evidence of N+1 queries in current implementation - Python code does not loop with embedded queries. However, lack of JOINs means multi-table analytics require multiple round-trips. | agent=research; confidence=0.9; joins_found=0; query_pattern=single_table_with_filters; reasoning={\"how\": \"Searched for JOIN keyword (0 results), analyzed query_entries method structure (single SELECT with dynamic WHERE), checked list_projects (no metrics joined), verified no for-loop query patterns.\", \"what\": \"Zero JOIN operations across entire storage layer. All queries: SELECT columns FROM single_table WHERE filters ORDER BY ts_iso. Common pattern: project_id filter + timestamp sort. No N+1 detected (no loops with embedded queries). Multi-table data requires multiple queries instead of JOIN.\", \"why\": \"Query patterns determine optimization strategy - JOINs require different indexing than filtered single-table queries\"}; stage=research; priority=low; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/scribe_codebase_audit/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-01-23T05:37:23.718191+00:00_e773aade", "timestamp": "2026-01-23T05:37:23.718262+00:00"}
{"op": "commit", "ref_id": "2026-01-23T05:37:23.718191+00:00_e773aade", "timestamp": "2026-01-23T05:37:23.730383+00:00"}
{"op": "append", "content": "[\u2705] [2026-01-23 05:37:38 UTC] [Agent: ResearchAgent-Database] [Project: scribe_codebase_audit] DATABASE RESEARCH INVESTIGATION COMPLETE. Seven areas analyzed: (1) Schema: 20 tables, 21 indexes documented, (2) Missing indexes: agent/emoji columns unindexed on scribe_entries, (3) Connection management: NO POOLING in SQLite - opens/closes per query, (4) Query patterns: Single-table only, no JOINs, no N+1, (5) Migration system: Clean idempotent design with _ensure_column/_ensure_index, (6) Data volume: scribe_entries grows unbounded, 3 tables have cleanup, (7) Postgres gap: 260 lines vs 2666, missing 55+ methods, HAS pooling. Ready to create comprehensive research document with optimization roadmap. | agent=research; areas_analyzed=7; confidence=1; reasoning={\"how\": \"Systematic investigation using scribe.read_file for code analysis, structure extraction for method counting, line_range inspection for implementation details, search for pattern discovery. Cross-referenced SQLite vs Postgres implementations.\", \"what\": \"Comprehensive analysis across 7 investigation areas covering schema, performance, architecture, compatibility. Key findings: no connection pooling (critical), missing indexes (high), unbounded growth (medium), Postgres incomplete (high). All findings verified with file:line references.\", \"why\": \"Complete investigation provides foundation for Architect to design database optimization strategy\"}; stage=research; total_findings=15; priority=medium; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/scribe_codebase_audit/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-01-23T05:37:38.605117+00:00_1939163e", "timestamp": "2026-01-23T05:37:38.605162+00:00"}
